--TEST--
`new $cls(...)` over a `class<T>` allocates whichever implementor the reference holds, through either of `as`'s two doors (`rule:types/class-reference` and `rule:types/class-reference-sites`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A class reference has exactly one source, `as`, and it has two doors: a
// `Formatter::class` operand is decided where it is written, and a string is
// walked at run time against the classes this unit declares to be
// `Formatter`s. `new` over the result allocates the class the descriptor
// holds and never the bound, which the overridden `render` is here to report.
class Formatter {
    public function constructor(public string $prefix) {}

    public function render(string $body): string {
        return $this->prefix . $body;
    }
}

class Angled extends Formatter {
    public function render(string $body): string {
        return $this->prefix . "<" . $body . ">";
    }
}

// Inherits both the constructor and `render`, so it is the implementor that
// answers with the bound's own body.
class Quoted extends Formatter {
}

// The compile-time door: `Dog::class as class<Animal>` in § 2's own words.
class<Formatter> $folded = Angled::class as class<Formatter>;
Formatter $one = new $folded("1: ");
echo $one->render("body"), "\n";

// The run-time door. The name is a `string` local rather than a literal, so
// nothing about it is known before the conversion runs.
string $name = "Quoted";
class<Formatter> $walked = $name as class<Formatter>;
Formatter $two = new $walked("2: ");
echo $two->render("body"), "\n";

// The bound is an implementor of its own reference — `class<T>` holds `T` as
// readily as it holds a subclass of it.
class<Formatter> $base = Formatter::class as class<Formatter>;
Formatter $three = new $base("3: ");
echo $three->render("body"), "\n";

// The arguments are checked against `T`'s constructor (§ 4), and they reach
// the allocated class's own — here inherited, which is the common case § 5's
// refusal leaves standing.
class<Formatter> $again = Angled::class as class<Formatter>;
echo (new $again("4: "))->render("body"), "\n";
// covers: lang:classes/inheritance
--EXPECT--
1: <body>
2: body
3: body
4: <body>
